WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.244
OpenTelemetry collects the traces, metrics and logs of an Orchard Core application, and

00:00:06.244 --> 00:00:09.119
tags them with the tenant that produced them.

00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:13.574
In this video, you enable it, read its telemetry in the Aspire dashboard,

00:00:13.574 --> 00:00:15.660
and configure what it collects.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:29.070
OpenTelemetry collects traces, metrics and logs from Orchard Core, ASP.NET Core, HttpClient and the .NET runtime.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:34.771
It exports them over OTLP to any compatible backend, such as the Aspire

00:00:34.771 --> 00:00:37.880
dashboard, Grafana or Azure Monitor.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:43.944
Spans, log records and HTTP server metrics carry the name of their tenant

00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:47.105
in the orchardcore.tenant.name attribute.

00:00:47.105 --> 00:00:54.033
Resource attributes, such as service.instance.id, identify each instance, so tenants and web farm

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.320
instances can be told apart.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.434
Unlike a module feature, it isn't enabled per tenant.

00:01:00.434 --> 00:01:04.824
Its listeners are global to the process, a request is traced before its

00:01:04.824 --> 00:01:09.214
tenant is known, a rebuilt tenant would reset its metrics, and the host

00:01:09.214 --> 00:01:11.210
decides where telemetry goes.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:22.218
Reference the OrchardCore.OpenTelemetry package, and call AddOpenTelemetry on the Orchard Core builder.

00:01:22.218 --> 00:01:25.840
Telemetry is collected even when nothing exports it.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.150
An optional callback adds other exporters and instrumentations.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:37.639
The OrchardCore.Cms.Web application exports telemetry only when an endpoint is configured, so it

00:01:37.639 --> 00:01:41.669
checks HasOtlpEndpoint before calling AddOpenTelemetry.

00:01:41.669 --> 00:01:44.840
Without an endpoint, nothing is collected.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.939
You set the endpoint of the OTLP receiver in the Otlp section of

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:59.010
the OrchardCore OpenTelemetry configuration, or with the standard OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT value, for example as an environment variable.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.702
For this video, the application sends its telemetry to the Aspire dashboard, with

00:02:08.702 --> 00:02:11.164
web-1 as its service instance ID.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.932
It serves two tenants:

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:16.720
Default, and Marketing under the marketing URL prefix.

00:02:18.376 --> 00:02:20.254
Let's open the Default tenant.

00:02:20.254 --> 00:02:23.488
Its home page is served at the root of the site, and the

00:02:23.488 --> 00:02:25.626
request is traced like every other.

00:02:26.108 --> 00:02:29.301
The Marketing tenant answers under its own prefix.

00:02:29.301 --> 00:02:34.034
The same application serves both tenants, and each request is tagged with the

00:02:34.034 --> 00:02:35.688
tenant that handled it.

00:02:39.899 --> 00:02:44.333
In the dashboard, the traces list the requests of both tenants, and the

00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:46.694
work Orchard Core does around them.

00:02:46.694 --> 00:02:51.129
Their resource is the application, named by its service.name attribute.

00:02:51.584 --> 00:02:57.590
The spans carry the tenant name, so let's filter the traces on the orchardcore.tenant.name attribute.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.800
We choose the value Marketing, and apply the filter, to keep only the

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.494
requests that the Marketing tenant served.

00:03:04.949 --> 00:03:07.306
Opening a request shows its spans.

00:03:07.306 --> 00:03:13.199
The span of the request itself holds the orchardcore.tenant.name attribute, which Orchard Core

00:03:13.199 --> 00:03:15.969
adds once the request has been processed.

00:03:16.432 --> 00:03:18.806
Orchard Core adds spans of its own.

00:03:18.806 --> 00:03:22.801
This trace shows the recipe that set up the tenant, with a span

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:25.290
for the recipe and one for each step.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:30.212
Building and activating a tenant, and running a background task, get spans too.

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:35.111
A Render shape span appears for a shape slower than the slow shape threshold.

00:03:35.111 --> 00:03:38.644
Here the threshold is zero, so every shape of the page has a

00:03:38.644 --> 00:03:42.017
span, with the template or shape method that rendered it.

00:03:46.207 --> 00:03:51.431
In the metrics, http.server.request.duration records how long requests take.

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:58.377
It carries the orchardcore.tenant.name attribute, so the Default and Marketing tenants can be compared.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:04.341
The OrchardCore meter adds orchardcore.tenants, the number of tenants by state, and the

00:04:04.341 --> 00:04:07.412
durations of building and activating a tenant.

00:04:07.412 --> 00:04:11.309
The System.Runtime meter reports the health of the instance.

00:04:15.609 --> 00:04:21.369
The structured logs show the records written through ILogger, with their attributes and scopes.

00:04:21.369 --> 00:04:27.301
Records written while a tenant handles a request carry the orchardcore.tenant.name attribute, here

00:04:27.301 --> 00:04:29.069
with the value Marketing.

00:04:29.538 --> 00:04:34.993
The log levels of the application's Logging configuration apply to these records.

00:04:34.993 --> 00:04:40.699
Setting Enabled to false in the Logging options of OpenTelemetry stops the collection

00:04:40.699 --> 00:04:44.336
of logs only, and leaves traces and metrics running.

00:04:44.336 --> 00:04:46.718
A separate video covers logging.

00:04:50.918 --> 00:04:55.296
The options are read from the OrchardCore OpenTelemetry section.

00:04:55.296 --> 00:05:00.486
ServiceName and ServiceInstanceId identify the application and this instance.

00:05:00.486 --> 00:05:06.301
SamplingRatio keeps a fraction of the traces, SlowShapeThreshold sets when a shape gets

00:05:06.301 --> 00:05:09.928
a span, and IncludeTenantName tags the HTTP metrics.

00:05:10.678 --> 00:05:16.090
OpenTelemetry collects only the activity sources and meters it is told about.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:22.153
Add YesSql to the Tracing Sources and Metrics Meters settings to see database commands.

00:05:22.153 --> 00:05:26.978
A session running many commands reveals an N plus one query pattern.

00:05:27.728 --> 00:05:32.896
To find slow templates, add the OrchardCore.DisplayManagement source and meter.

00:05:32.896 --> 00:05:37.395
Every rendered shape then gets a span, nested like the shapes, and a

00:05:37.395 --> 00:05:41.408
histogram records the rendering duration of each shape type.

00:05:42.158 --> 00:05:47.686
For a hosted backend such as Grafana Cloud, the protocol is http/protobuf, and

00:05:47.686 --> 00:05:50.319
the headers carry the credentials.

00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:53.938
Setting Enabled to false turns all telemetry off.

00:05:58.138 --> 00:06:03.964
When the application already configures OpenTelemetry, for example with the service defaults of

00:06:03.964 --> 00:06:10.077
an Aspire solution, call AddOrchardCoreInstrumentation on the tracing, metrics and logging builders.

00:06:10.077 --> 00:06:12.038
Add it before the exporters.

00:06:12.788 --> 00:06:19.879
A module emits its own spans with the OrchardCoreTelemetry ActivitySource, from OrchardCore.Abstractions.

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:25.244
RecordException marks a failed operation, and a span started in a tenant scope

00:06:25.244 --> 00:06:27.288
is tagged with its tenant.

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:34.168
OpenTelemetry tags the traces, metrics and logs of an Orchard Core application with

00:06:34.168 --> 00:06:37.513
their tenant, and sends them to the backend you choose.

00:06:37.513 --> 00:06:38.938
Thanks for watching.

